{:edb/db-before-t 338 :edb/db-after-t 339 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533651 50 #inst "2026-10-04T06:51:55.104Z" 13194139533651 true] [17592186124390 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244186 13194139533651 true] [17592186124390 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244221 13194139533651 true] [17592186124390 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244228 13194139533651 true] [17592186124390 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244235 13194139533651 true] [17592186124390 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244242 13194139533651 true] [17592186124391 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244193 13194139533651 true] [17592186124391 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244200 13194139533651 true] [17592186124391 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244207 13194139533651 true] [17592186124391 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244214 13194139533651 true] [17592186124392 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244249 13194139533651 true] [17592186124392 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244256 13194139533651 true] [17592186124392 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244263 13194139533651 true] [17592186124937 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533651 true] [17592186124937 1145 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-2,0].$ Indeed, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/d7c6e8d314483ad67e89ce5d4675b7af27fa08c88a4ed9d95eed9ad2a4da51cc.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the intervals $x\\in (-2,2]$ and $x\\in [-4,-2]\\cup [2,4]$ because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/cee829f7e3d5f32b0acb54cb5c40406d8391d2a2967c68df0c1f5e1cae87d729.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533651 true] [17592186125111 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533651 true] [17592186125111 1145 "The graph of the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units up to get $f(x)={x}^{2}+2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/ff3d1ac7500d83574b8671b23e99d4a0f1dbd6ab9cebc906aa06ff2c21f95345.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-2,2).$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/a71ce036adc5dc0b15fce9e367a0c8512699dc6a7814f4e9a1886696d2abad6e.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the intervals $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ and  $x\\in (-\\infty ,0].$ On these intervals, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/6858bd1fab8d9659c84fa941cd63ee9f509c8364dce0e753706c640ecfe6e9ec.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139533651 true] [17592186125112 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533651 true] [17592186125112 1145 "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $1$ and $y$-intercept $1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/00d6394343d441ee10fa20ee029b8f89b52ff1e6ded8e7fdbe0756118212f1a8.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ it is also invertible on  $x\\in (-1,\\infty )$ and $x\\in (-10,5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139533651 true] [17592186244186 1017 #edb/ref 17592186244187 13194139533651 true] [17592186244186 1089 "q-108581" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244186 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533651 true] [17592186244186 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/89b0be0ca7ae1a6e76d32bfc669d6f554deaaad2055b2875e3f6a49821043205.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244186 1113 #uuid "738dd895-faa9-59f9-902b-04ed647fcfd6" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244186 1138 false 13194139533651 true] [17592186244186 1145 "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/cfb095836b7498c91a5cbd9d2cb694715a0d89a12a5e6ea5ff417342c4bde798.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graph III, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph III shows a one-to-one function. As a result, this function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, II, and IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible." 13194139533651 true] [17592186244187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244188 13194139533651 true] [17592186244187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244189 13194139533651 true] [17592186244187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244190 13194139533651 true] [17592186244187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244191 13194139533651 true] [175