ible on the interval $x\\in (-3,1)$  because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/9ea54918e2ef4310b58e9cf8a0c9b1f9757a404d97dbbfd9e5b037af2193f38c.png)\n\n \n\nHence, the correct answer is 'I and III only''." 13194139533651 true] [17592186244201 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244202 13194139533651 true] [17592186244201 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244203 13194139533651 true] [17592186244201 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244204 13194139533651 true] [17592186244201 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244205 13194139533651 true] [17592186244201 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244206 13194139533651 true] [17592186244201 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533651 true] [17592186244201 1041 "selection" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244201 1104 #edb/ref 17592186244203 13194139533651 true] [17592186244201 1110 #uuid "09e52a2a-b6fb-5231-b075-aecb8b2b042a" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244202 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244202 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533651 true] [17592186244202 1111 #uuid "00bf83c4-61fd-509d-b03a-3081edca0abf" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244203 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244203 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533651 true] [17592186244203 1111 #uuid "7b3ccbba-7692-5b9f-8f6a-d2f872405d99" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244204 1034 "I only" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244204 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533651 true] [17592186244204 1111 #uuid "30f273d6-04c3-5a35-bff9-f86644a94b9d" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244205 1034 "III only" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244205 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533651 true] [17592186244205 1111 #uuid "ce204ac1-108e-5ada-abc7-89ed9f70b592" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244206 1034 "II only" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244206 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533651 true] [17592186244206 1111 #uuid "91093f4e-b466-5a4d-a6b5-f81025a64891" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244207 1017 #edb/ref 17592186244208 13194139533651 true] [17592186244207 1089 "q-36004" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244207 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533651 true] [17592186244207 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/1597ad53471d0d9b44812c5caa31bcb160cfa150d1df9a9314447fa49e62ccff.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $x\\in [-6,0]$\n- $x\\in [-6,4)$\n- $x\\in [4,10)$" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244207 1113 #uuid "f1576eb0-9e79-5e65-81b7-76d09de9784a" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244207 1138 false 13194139533651 true] [17592186244207 1145 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n- The given function is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in [-6,0]$ and $x\\in [4,10).$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/7cb282e3fa66af801ff03816973d5f5f1d0cb41e39d08a8addd27d7aa9c5b9df.png)\n\n      \n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the interval $[-6,4]$  because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/000953609a7b3e13ad09473ead62f96aa895c7f94aa618884e69883595004fd4.png)\n\n \n\n Therefore, the correct answer is \"I  and III only\"." 13194139533651 true] [17592186244208 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244209 13194139533651 true] [17592186244208 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244210 13194139533651 true] [17592186244208 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244211 13194139533651 true] [17592186244208 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244212 13194139533651 true] [17592186244208 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244213 13194139533651 tru