n is $x\\in [-3,4).$\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ does not pass the horizontal line test on the interval $x\\in [1,4).$ In this case, the horizontal line $y=-1$ intersects the graph at more than one point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/a709558673202ef4a7835dcea6e23b7ec5b13684272c56023058223c01fb8061.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in [-3,1].$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/5a65e71f571e9510580ba5fe105a6eac48acfe4564c5a6bf76371c2bf09ff651.png)\n\nTherefore, the largest interval on which the function is invertible is:\n\n$$\nx\\in [-3,1]\n$$" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244215 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244216 13194139533651 true] [17592186244215 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244217 13194139533651 true] [17592186244215 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244218 13194139533651 true] [17592186244215 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244219 13194139533651 true] [17592186244215 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244220 13194139533651 true] [17592186244215 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533651 true] [17592186244215 1041 "selection" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244215 1104 #edb/ref 17592186244218 13194139533651 true] [17592186244215 1110 #uuid "92d8ecc6-9335-5095-9a7d-fdf6881b1c23" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244216 1034 "x\\in [-3,4)" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244216 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533651 true] [17592186244216 1111 #uuid "1e3f621d-87a9-55d3-955b-17f9e012a4be" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244217 1034 "x\\in [-3,0]" 13194139533651 true] [17592186244217 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533651 true] [17592186244217